// Where a word starts and stops — UAX #29 word boundaries.
//
// Splitting on spaces gets English mostly right and everything else
// wrong: `can't` is one word and `1,000.50` is one number, while Japanese
// has no spaces at all. A search index, a spell checker, a double-click
// selection and a `wc -w` all want the same answer, and it is not
// `split(" ")`.
//
// ## The rules that are not local
//
//   - **A separator is only a separator with letters on both sides.**
//     `can't` is one word; `'quoted'` is three, because the quotes have
//     nothing on the far side. So the decision at the apostrophe depends
//     on the character *after* it.
//   - **Marks disappear.** A combining accent, a format character or a
//     zero-width joiner attaches to what came before and the rules look
//     straight through it. Two letters with an accent between them are
//     still two letters in a row.
//   - **Regional indicators pair off**, exactly as they do for grapheme
//     clusters, and for the same reason.
//
// ## What this is not
//
// A tokeniser. It says where the boundaries are; deciding which of the
// pieces are words — as opposed to spaces and punctuation — is `words`,
// and deciding what a word *means* is somebody else's package.
//
// The table comes from `WordBreakProperty.txt` for Unicode 17.0.0, and
// `graphemes` supplies the pictograph test that rule WB3c needs.
import graphemes

